 "withdraw": {
  "eureka-quadcopter": {
   "decision": "withdrawn: fails the noise gate (human against zero reward below 1 pooled seed standard deviation at every budget tried; formal gate at 200 x 48 x 2, the largest budget whose worst case fits the 900 s cap)",
   "reason": "In the CPU MuJoCo port of IsaacGymEnvs Quadcopter (same MJCF, actions, resets and fitness, -mean distance to (0, 0, 1)), a CEM-trained linear policy does not learn to fly to the target within budgets that fit the 900 s timeout: Eureka's human reward does not separate from a zero reward (formal gate below: -1.438 +- 0.117 against -1.525 +- 0.108 over training seeds 0-4, 0.78 pooled seed standard deviations where 2 are required; it does beat the alive bonus by 3.16), and only 3 of 64 trained policies beat the policy that outputs zero actions (-1.283 on the same 50 evaluation episodes). Likely cause: thrusts are integrated from actions (thrust += 0.01 * 200 * action, clamped to [0, 2] N) but are not part of Isaac's 21-number observation, and in a simplified vertical model a static linear feedback on height and vertical speed cannot stabilise this integrator chain (smallest spectral radius 1.0 over a grid of gains, local analysis); Isaac trains an MLP policy (256-256-128 units) with PPO on 8,192 GPU environments for 3,000 iterations, which can learn nonlinear, switching control. Making the policy observe the thrusts or widening the CEM search did not help at 100 x 32 x 2; one seed of a 300 x 48 x 2 run (about 600 s) reached -0.60 while another stayed at -1.52, so even a budget at the cap would leave the score dominated by training-seed luck.",
